 Cluster permission revision synchronization is opt-in at build time with `-p:EnableClusterIntegration=true`. Cluster instances require the per-instance `LANCER_NEXUS_GAME_INSTANCE_KEY` and the internal Redis `LANCER_NEXUS_PERMISSION_REDIS_ENDPOINT=host:port`. LLServer reports `IsReady=false` until it has subscribed to Redis, loaded and activated the current Gateway SQL permission snapshot, and received its revision ACK. Redis carries invalidations only; a disconnected listener makes the instance unready. Standalone builds remain independent of Cluster.
 
-Clustered character creation registers the exact display name and initial SaveGame with Gateway under the active account/session; a Gateway rejection rolls back the local compatibility row. LLServer loads the authoritative Gateway roster, reconstructs missing local cache rows only from validated Gateway snapshots, and deletes through Gateway's history-preserving tombstone before removing a local row. A legacy character without a Gateway snapshot is available only on instances that still have its local SQLite row; missing legacy state is omitted instead of replaced with a default pilot. After selection, LLServer acquires the fenced lease and loads the latest Gateway SaveGame snapshot. If no snapshot exists yet, it imports the matching local SQLite row once as revision 1. LLServer checkpoints the live SaveGame to Gateway every minute, persists explicit Freelancer saves, and writes a transferred SaveGame under the target lease fence after transfer commit. Gateway validates each read and write against the active instance, session, lease token and version. On graceful `StopServer`, LLServer rejects new logins, drains active login work, pauses world simulation, captures each active leased character on the simulation loop, and waits for the final Gateway snapshot write before stopping the loop. Characters already in transfer use the transfer snapshot protocol. A forced process kill cannot run the shutdown checkpoint; local SQLite remains a compatibility/bootstrap cache, not the cluster persistence authority.
